illion dollars worth  of products that americans consume  president trump reached a ceasefire with  china earlier this year  ultimately it's a great deal for both  countries and it's going to also lead to  even a more stable piece  throughout the world the coronavirus has  added a new  chill to the bond between the world's  two largest economies  it's going to make it hard to reach new  deals with america's big  economic rival the united states wants  an open and constructive relationship  with china but achieving that  relationship  requires us to vigorously defend our  national interests  mr trump has also challenged american  allies like canada  mexico and germany biden won't move fast  to take those  china tariffs away but he does want to  repair alliances with other countries  last week i shared the outlines of my  plan to  build back better that plan rejects  the defeatist view that automation and  globalization  mean we can't ensure our future is made  in america on energy in the environment  the incumbent and his challenger are  also worlds apart  and that reflects a much different set  of concerns within their own parties  during his first term president trump  pulled out of the paris agreement to  reduce emissions and combat climate  change  instead the president has prioritized  domestic fuel production  and sped up approvals of new pipelines  and other energy infrastructure  his policies are meant to support jobs  in energy producing regions like the  appalachians  in west texas police is hurt by the  recent recession  we are telling the washington  politicians trying to abolish american  energy  don't mess with texas  if re-elected the president wants to  roll back regulations further  biden on the other hand wants to rejoin  the paris agreement  and make new commitments to reduce u.s  carbon emissions  that includes regulations to cut  commercial building emissions by 2030.  biden also has a two trillion dollar  spending plan to invest in areas like  s